<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Komnenos Asen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1363">1363</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Komnenos Asen was a Bulgarian noble and brother-in-law of the Serbian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>, who made him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Valona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanine">Kanina</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> after taking them around 1345. After Dušan's death in 1355 he ruled them as an independent despot, also gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a>, until he died of plague in 1363.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Komnenos Asen came from the highest ranks of 14th-century Bulgarian nobility. His mother, Keratsa Petritsa, was a daughter of the despot Shishman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> and a descendant of Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a>, and his father, Sratsimir, was despot of Kran. His brother Ivan Alexander took the Bulgarian throne in 1331, and his sister Helena married Stephen Dušan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 1332. His birth date is unknown.</p><p>Why he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> instead of seeking high office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> is uncertain. The Bulgarian historian Ivan Bozhilov holds that he was not seeking refuge but most likely expected Serbia's expansion to offer him a better career; presumably he travelled with Helena in 1332. Dušan made him a despot, most likely in 1345 or 1346, when Dušan was proclaimed emperor. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-uros">Simeon Uroš</a> and Jovan Oliver he was one of three men to hold that title under Dušan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was installed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Valona</a> (modern Vlorë) in late 1345, after the Serbian conquest of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The extent of his lands is uncertain: estimates range from all of central Albania to a few towns, with the rest held by local Albanian nobles. In 1349 he plundered a wrecked Venetian merchant ship under the medieval principle of jus naufragii, and Dušan had to settle the dispute. Even so, Valona traded actively with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and Ragusa (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>), and receipts of 1350 show him drawing a large income from the port's customs.</p><p>In 1353 he and his family received Venetian citizenship. In the civil war that followed Dušan's death he backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-uros">Simeon Uroš</a>, his wife's son-in-law, against Dušan's son Stephen Uroš. Simeon failed, but John kept his lands and became independent of both. Threatened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-orsini">Nikephoros II Orsini</a>, he asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> for a warship and an administrator, and the republic sent them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The last record of his activity is a commercial document of 30 January 1359. His death was not recorded, but it is likely that he died in the plague that struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Valona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a> (modern Durrës) in 1363. Around 1350–1355 he had married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-savoy">Anna Palaiologina</a>, a granddaughter of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> and widow of John II Orsini of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. His successor at Valona, Alexander Komnenos Asen, was probably his son by an unknown first wife.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Komnenos Asen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His brother-in-law <b>John Komnenos Asen</b> governed them, and after Dušan's death in 1355 ruled as an independent despot.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-valona">Principality of Valona</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Second Bulgarian Empire. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.31,19.45&amp;year=1353" aria-label="Durrës on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5025 690 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of John Komnenos Asen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5475 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5475 960h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.31,19.45&amp;year=1353">What was here in 1353? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
